1548:
(a) Prior to the installation of any water or sewerage system (hereinafter referred to as "utility system") in any subdivision or multifamily development hereafter developed or constructed in the city, the plans for such system shall be submitted to the superintendent or inspector of the water, sewerage and light commission. Once the approval of the superintendent or inspector is obtained, the owner/developer of the proposed subdivision or multifamily development shall be permitted to begin construction of such utility system; provided, however, the developer and/or owner must either:
(1) Elect for the water, sewerage and light commission to supervise the construction of the utility system by requesting the commission to take charge of all facets of the construction project from the invitation for bids to the final approval, all on behalf of the owner/developer. If this alternative is selected, the following procedure shall be adhered to:
a. The owner/developer shall ask that the commission make an estimate of the total cost of the project;
b. Once such an estimate is obtained, the owner/developer will deposit in an escrow account approved by the commission an amount of funds sufficient to pay such estimated costs;
c. The commission will then publicly advertise for bids for the construction of such project;
d. When the bids are received, the commission shall select the lowest and best bid;
e. If the estimated cost is not sufficient to pay the bid price selected, the owner/developer will deposit such additional funds into escrow as are necessary to cover the bid price and all other costs of construction including supervision, if any;
f. The bidder chosen will then be awarded the contract and will perform the construction of the project under the supervision of the commission until the utility system is installed in accordance with the approved plans and final approval of system is given by the commission; or
(2) Elect that the construction of the utility system will be done by a contractor selected, paid and supervised by the owner/developer, but with additional direct supervision by a representative of the water, sewerage and light commission, the total cost of which supervision shall be paid by the owner/developer to the commission. The owner/developer may elect this alternative (2) in lieu of alternative (1) prior to any of the steps enumerated in alternative (1) above, but shall pay all costs incurred by commission up to that point prior to being allowed to utilize this alternative (2).
(b) In either event, no person shall be allowed to occupy or inhabit subdivision or the multifamily development until such time as the final approval of the utility system as installed is given by the proper representative of the water, sewerage and light commission.
(Code 1972, § 26-78)

1575:
Any person violating any provision of any section of this division shall be tried before the municipal court of the city. Upon conviction, a violation of this ordinance may be punished in accordance with the following schedule:
(1) It shall be a separate offense under this division for each day that a person or entity knowingly violates any section of any provision of this division, or continues to violate any section of any provision of this division.
(2) Persons committing offenses under this division, within a calendar year, shall:
a. On the first violation, receive a verbal notice of violation;
b. On the second violation, receive written notice of violation;
c. On the third violation, be fined $100.00;
d. On the fourth violation, be fined $250.00;
e. On the fifth violation, the Newnan Utilities may disconnect water services to customer and assess reconnection fees and additional monetary penalties to the customer.
(Ord. No. 06-92, § 6(b), 3-23-92; Ord. No. 17-21, § 1, 10-24-17)

1576:
On or after April 1, 1992, no construction may be initiated within the city for any residential building of any type which:
(1) Employs a gravity tank type, flushometer-valve or flushometer-tank toilet that uses more than an average of 1.6 gallons of water per flush; provided, however, this paragraph shall not be applicable to one-piece toilets until July 1, 1992;
(2) Employs a showerhead that allows a flow of more than an average of 2.5 gallons of water per minute at 60 pounds per square inch of pressure;
(3) Employs a urinal that uses more than an average of 1.0 gallons of water per flush;
(4) Employs a lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute; or
(5) Employs a kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.5 gallons of water per minute.
(Ord. No. 06-92, § 2, 3-23-92)

1578:
(a) New construction and the repair or renovation of an existing building shall be exempt from the requirements of this division when:
(1) The repair or renovation of the existing building does not include the replacement of the plumbing or sewage system servicing toilets, faucets or showerheads within such existing buildings;
(2) When such plumbing or sewage system within such existing building, because of its capacity, design or installation would not function properly if the toilets, faucets or showerheads required by this division were installed;
(3) Such system is a well or gravity flow from a spring and is owned privately by an individual for use in such individual's personal residence; or
(4) Units to be installed are:
a. Specifically designed for use by the handicapped;
b. Specifically designed to withstand unusual abuse of installation in a penal institution; or
c. Toilets of juveniles.
(b) The owner, or his agent, of a building undergoing new construction or repair or renovation who is entitled to an exemption as specified in subsection (a)(2), (3) or (4) of this section shall obtain the exemption by applying at the office of the building official for the city. A fee of $25.00 shall be charged for the inspection and issuance of such exemption.
(Ord. No. 06-92, § 5, 3-23-92)

1580:
(a) Outdoor watering for purposes of planting, growing, managing, or maintaining ground cover, trees, shrubs, or other plants may occur only between the hours of 4:00 p.m. and 10:00 a.m.; provided, however, that this limitation shall not create any limitation upon the following outdoor water uses:
(1) Commercial raising, harvesting, or storing of crops; feeding, breeding, or managing livestock or poultry; the commercial production or storing of feed for use in the production of livestock, including, but not limited to, cattle, calves, swine, hogs, goats, sheep, and rabbits, or for use in the production of poultry, including, but not limited to, chickens, hens, ratites, and turkeys; producing plants, trees, fowl, or animals; or the commercial production of aquacultural, horticultural, dairy, livestock, poultry, eggs, and apiarian products or as otherwise defined in O.C.G.A. § 1-3-3;
(2) Capture and reuse of cooling system condensate or storm water in compliance with applicable city ordinances and state guidelines;
(3) Reuse of gray water in compliance with O.C.G.A. § 31-3-5.2 and applicable local board of health regulations;
(4) Use of reclaimed waste water by a designated user from a system permitted by the Environmental Protection Division of the Georgia Department of Natural Resources to provide reclaimed waste water;
(5) Watering personal food gardens;
(6) Watering new and replanted plant, seed, or turf in landscapes, golf courses, or sports turf fields during installation and for a period of 30 days immediately following the date of installation;
(7) Drip irrigation or irrigation using soaker hoses;
(8) Hand watering with a hose with automatic cutoff or handheld container;
(9) Use of water withdrawn from private water wells or surface water by an owner or operator of property if such well or surface water is on said property;
(10) Watering horticultural crops held for sale, resale, or installation;
(11) Watering athletic fields, golf courses, or public turf grass recreational areas;
(12) Installation, maintenance, or calibration of irrigation systems; or
(13) Hydroseeding.
(b) No person shall use or allow the use of water in violation of the restrictions on outdoor water use contained in this section.
(c) Enforcement of this section and penalty for violation of the provisions of this section shall be as provided in sections 21-152, 21-153, and 21-157(e) and (f) of this division.
(d) The provisions of this section shall be in addition to the authority granted in section 21-157 of the City's Code of Ordinances to the Newnan Water Sewerage and Light Commission to provide for water conservation measures as provided for therein.
(Ord. No. 10-20, § I, 11-23-10, eff. 1-1-11)

1582:
(a) In addition to the restrictions on use of water set forth in the other sections of this division 3, the following uses are a waste or unreasonable use or method of use of potable water and are prohibited:
(1) Allowing water from an irrigation system or other lawn or landscaping watering device to escape from any premises onto public right-of-way, such as streets and sidewalks, or upon any other person's property.
(2) Operating an irrigation system or other lawn or landscaping watering device during any form of precipitation.
(3) Operating an irrigation system or other lawn or landscaping watering device that has any broken or missing sprinkler heads.
(4) Failing to repair a controllable leak, including a broken sprinkler head, or a leaking valve, or a leaking outdoor faucet, or a service line leak, or any other visible outside water leaks, on premises owned, leased, or managed by that person, within 30 days.
(b) The restrictions in paragraph (a) do not apply to the following:
(1) Flow resulting from firefighting or routine inspection of fire hydrants or from fire training activities;
(2) Water applied as a dust control measure;
(3) Water applied to abate spills of flammable or otherwise hazardous materials, where water is the appropriate methodology;
(4) Water applied to prevent or abate health, safety. or accident hazards when alternate methods are not available;
(5) Flow resulting from routine inspection, operation, or maintenance of traffic flow control devices;
(6) Water used for construction or maintenance activities where the application of water is appropriate methodology and where no other practical alternative exists.
(7) The provisions of this section shall be enforced as provided in sections 21-152, 21-153, 21-157(e) and (f) of this division 3.
(Ord. No. 17-21, § 3, 10-24-17)
